(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-1-dermatological-infestations-vignettes-version-2"]={"title":"Dermatological Infestations Vignettes 2 — CMS I Exam 1","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 78-year-old nursing home resident has widespread thick flaking scale on the hands, feet and trunk with thickened discoloured nails. He does not complain of itch. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Crusted scabies","Ordinary scabies","Chronic plaque psoriasis","Asteatotic eczema of age"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — a massive mite burden, poorly defined patches, often no pruritus, and highly infectious. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"A 34-year-old woman treated for scabies two months ago has itching again, and this time it began three days after her partner was diagnosed. Which best explains the timing?","choices":["Delusional parasitosis, in which the patient feels mites that are no longer present","Reinfestation, in which symptoms develop within two to three days rather than weeks","Treatment failure, in which symptoms never resolved from the original infestation","Post-scabietic pruritus, which persists for up to four weeks after successful therapy"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the first infestation takes four to six weeks because sensitisation must occur. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"A 41-year-old woman with bedbug bites has scratched several open and one is now crusted and tender. Which is the most appropriate treatment?","choices":["Overnight topical permethrin to the whole skin surface with a repeat dose at one week","Surgical excision of the affected lesions followed by tetanus prophylaxis","Topical antiseptic lotion or antibiotic cream for the secondary infection, with local wound care","An oral antibiotic covering methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus for ten days"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — topical steroids or oral antihistamines address the pruritus separately. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"A 45-year-old rice farmer working in flooded paddies has recurrent itchy papular eruptions on his legs each season. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Tungiasis from walking barefoot in contaminated soil","Cutaneous larva migrans from soil contaminated with faeces","Body louse infestation from prolonged wear of the same clothing","Cercarial dermatitis from repeated exposure to infested water"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — paddy workers and rice farmers of the Far East are specifically named. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 89"},{"q":"A 35-year-old woman treated for scabies three weeks ago still has itchy papules, though no new burrows are seen and her household has been treated. Which is the most appropriate treatment?","choices":["Oral antibiotics directed at staphylococcal superinfection of the excoriations","A mid to high potency topical corticosteroid, or intralesional triamcinolone acetonide","A second full course of permethrin applied overnight to the whole skin surface","Oral ivermectin every two weeks for two to three doses alongside topical therapy"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — persistent post-scabietic papules are a recognised complication. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"A 38-year-old man asks how bedbugs got into his flat when he keeps it clean. Which is the most appropriate explanation?","choices":["They spread in the clothing and baggage of travellers and visitors, and in second-hand mattresses and laundry","They spread by direct skin-to-skin contact lasting fifteen to twenty minutes with an infested person","They spread through contaminated clothing that cannot be washed or changed regularly","They spread from soil or sand contaminated with the faeces of dogs and cats"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the infestation is not a marker of poor hygiene. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"A 47-year-old man in rural Tennessee has had fever to 39.8 degrees Celsius and severe headache for four days. Today a blanching macular rash appeared on his ankles and wrists, including the palms and soles, sparing his face. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Rocky Mountain spotted fever","Early Lyme disease with erythema migrans","A drug eruption from a recently started medication","Meningococcaemia with an early petechial rash"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the triad of fever, headache and rash, with centripetal spread involving palms and soles. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 82"},{"q":"The parent of an 8-year-old treated for head lice asks when she can return to school. Which is the most appropriate response?","choices":["She must stay home until every single nit has been combed out of her hair completely","She must stay home for two full weeks after the treatment has been completed","She must stay home until a repeat wet comb finds no nits and no live lice","She can return now — a no-nit policy is not recommended because of the school absence it causes"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — that position comes from the American Academy of Pediatrics. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"A 22-year-old man who keeps a pet tarantula has an itchy papular rash on his forearms and a red, gritty eye. Which is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Oral antihistamine for the skin, with artificial tears and review in two weeks","Oral ivermectin for the skin, with topical antibiotic drops for the affected eye","Surgical removal of each embedded hair from the skin and from the eye surface","Topical corticosteroid for the skin, with ophthalmology referral for the eye"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — shed hairs embed in skin and eyes, causing conjunctivitis and corneal granuloma. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"A 26-year-old man has mild pubic itching, slate-grey to bluish irregular macules about 1 cm across on the lower abdomen, and papules around the umbilicus. Which additional step is most appropriate?","choices":["Screen for diabetes, since the intertriginous distribution suggests an underlying metabolic cause","Screen for immunosuppression, since the massive mite burden indicates impaired immunity","Screen for coeliac disease, since the eruption is associated with gluten sensitivity","Screen for concurrent sexually transmitted infection, since pubic lice often coexist with one"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the macules are maculae caeruleae, representing haemorrhage at feeding sites. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"A 12-year-old girl has an intensely painful sting on the arm with two parallel rows of purpura at the site after handling a caterpillar. Which caterpillar is responsible?","choices":["The asp or puss caterpillar, described as the most poisonous","The gypsy moth caterpillar, which causes caterpillar dermatitis","The processionary caterpillar, which causes systemic reactions","The tarantula, whose shed hairs embed in skin and in the eyes"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the train-track pattern of purpura is characteristic. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"A 47-year-old man has a nodular lesion on the toe suspected to be tungiasis. Which test confirms it?","choices":["Skin scraping with mineral oil examined under the microscope","Light microscopy with mineral oil looking for live larvae","Indirect immunofluorescence assay for specific antibodies","Dermoscopy, which visualises the ovoid eggs within the lesion"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — treatment is then surgical excision or cryotherapy. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"A 30-year-old pregnant woman has been in contact with a household member diagnosed with scabies but has no symptoms and no lesions. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Treat her only after delivery, whatever her symptoms or examination findings show","Withhold treatment, since pregnant patients are treated only where scabies is documented","Treat her with permethrin now, since all household contacts are treated regardless","Treat her with oral ivermectin now, since topical agents are avoided in pregnancy"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — treating all infested persons in the group applies to those with documented infestation. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"An 8-year-old boy brushed against a caterpillar in the garden and now has multiple itchy erythematous papules arranged in linear streaks on his forearm. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Cutaneous larva migrans acquired from soil in the same garden","Erucism, or caterpillar dermatitis, from a gypsy moth caterpillar","An asp or puss caterpillar sting, the most poisonous of the group","A processionary caterpillar reaction with urticaria and angio-oedema"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — a pruritic dermatitis with papules in linear streaks. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"A 45-year-old man has confirmed bedbug bites and asks what will get rid of them. Which is the most appropriate advice?","choices":["A professional exterminator is necessary; treatment of the bites themselves is symptomatic","Washing all bedding at 60 degrees Celsius and bagging clothing for fourteen days will clear it","Leaving the room unoccupied for a month will starve the insects out of the property","Vacuuming floors, carpets and upholstery thoroughly is sufficient without any chemical treatment"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — topical antiseptic or antibiotic for secondary infection, steroids or antihistamines for itch. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"A 33-year-old woman presents with a rash that shows discrete circumscribed papules and, in the older areas, crusting and scaling. Which conclusion follows from the crusting?","choices":["The lesions are resolving spontaneously","The subcutaneous tissue is involved","The epidermis has been affected","The infection is bacterial in origin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — crust and scale indicate epidermal involvement, which narrows the reaction pattern. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"A 33-year-old woman with a hobo spider bite asks what to expect. Which is the most appropriate counselling point?","choices":["The wound will need surgical reconstruction and healing will take several months","The symptoms will resolve within six hours with intravenous calcium gluconate","The lesion will continue to advance daily until antiparasitic treatment is given","The wound heals within several weeks and headaches may last a week; treatment is supportive"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — death from severe systemic effects, including aplastic anaemia, is rare. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"A 15-year-old boy has severe pain after an asp caterpillar sting that is not controlled by an oral analgesic. Which additional options are described?","choices":["Intramuscular epinephrine repeated at intervals until the pain has settled","A ten-day course of oral doxycycline to prevent secondary skin infection","Oral or parenteral narcotic analgesia, with antivenom for certain categories","Intravenous calcium gluconate with a muscle relaxant and a benzodiazepine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, alongside antihistamines, menthol or camphor, and corticosteroids. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"A 38-year-old woman in Missouri was bitten while cleaning an attic. The site now shows a dark purple centre surrounded by a pale blanched ring, and beyond that a large irregular area of redness. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Early cellulitis at the site","A brown recluse spider bite","A black widow spider bite","A hobo spider bite"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the red, white and blue sign is the hallmark of this envenomation. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"A 34-year-old man has a suspected serpentine skin lesion but the diagnosis is uncertain and follicular pustules are also present. Which test is most appropriate?","choices":["Dermoscopy of the lesion looking for the ovoid eggs within the affected skin","Bacterial culture of an unroofed pustule sent for Gram stain and sensitivities","Light microscopy with mineral oil, which shows live and dead larvae in folliculitis","Serology for immunoglobulin M and immunoglobulin G against the responsible organism"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the serpentine rash alone is enough for a clinical diagnosis. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"A 55-year-old man from a rural area has fever, severe headache, nausea and calf pain for five days but no rash. Which is the most appropriate consideration?","choices":["Rocky Mountain spotted fever can present without a rash in about 20% of patients","The absence of rash excludes the diagnosis, so an alternative should be sought","The rash always appears within twenty-four hours of the fever beginning","Treatment should be withheld until a rash confirms the clinical diagnosis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the full triad is present in only about 60% and the rash follows the fever. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 85"},{"q":"A 46-year-old man reached into a woodpile and felt a sharp bite. Within thirty minutes he has sweating and gooseflesh around the bite, and now has severe cramping abdominal pain and muscle spasms. Which spider is most likely responsible?","choices":["A tarantula","The black widow spider","The brown recluse spider","The hobo spider"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — alpha-latrotoxin produces this neurotoxic picture within thirty minutes. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"A 39-year-old man had anaphylaxis after a wasp sting last summer and has a positive skin test. Which is the most appropriate long-term management?","choices":["Avoid all outdoor activity between the months of July and September each year","Carry an epinephrine auto-injector and be referred for desensitisation therapy","Carry an oral antihistamine and take it at the first sign of any sting reaction","Take a daily leukotriene antagonist through the summer months of each year"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — immunotherapy is specified for fire ant hypersensitivity as well. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"A 9-year-old girl has early Lyme disease with erythema migrans. Which is the most appropriate oral antibiotic?","choices":["Doxycycline","Azithromycin","Ceftriaxone","Amoxicillin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — it is the alternative first line for early disease in children and pregnant patients. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 79"},{"q":"A 48-year-old man with Lyme arthritis of the knee has not responded to a full course of oral doxycycline. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Intra-articular corticosteroid injection into the affected knee","Intravenous ceftriaxone, cefotaxime or penicillin G","A second oral course of doxycycline for a further fortnight","Oral azithromycin, since a macrolide covers resistant organisms"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — intravenous therapy is used for arthritis and acrodermatitis chronica atrophicans. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 80"},{"q":"A 10-year-old boy's parents want to avoid chemical treatment for head lice. Which physical approach can be offered, and with what caveat?","choices":["Combing nits out after two minutes of hair moisturiser every few days, though it is slow and needs adjuvant therapy","Applying mayonnaise or mineral oil weekly to smother the live lice and their attached eggs on the hair","Washing the hair daily with an antibacterial shampoo until no live lice are seen","Waiting without treatment, since head lice clear spontaneously within a few weeks"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — head shaving is the other physical option named. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"A 42-year-old man with treated scabies develops spreading erythema, warmth and purulent crusting over several excoriated areas, with fever. Which is the most likely complication?","choices":["Delusions of ongoing parasitosis","A reaction to topical permethrin","Staphylococcal superinfection","Post-scabietic pruritic papules"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — it may lead to sepsis and requires antibiotic treatment. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"A 54-year-old man with suspected body lice has no visible organisms on skin examination. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Pluck a hair from the affected area and examine it under the microscope for nits","Scrape an unexcoriated papule with mineral oil and examine the smear microscopically","Examine the seams of his clothing for nits and shake the clothing over white paper","Wet comb the scalp with water and conditioner using a fine-toothed nit comb"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the lice will move onto the paper where they can be seen. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"A 17-year-old boy who swims in the same lake each summer asks how to reduce his risk of swimmer's itch. Which is the most appropriate advice?","choices":["Wear closed footwear on the shore and avoid sitting on the sand","Take a prophylactic antiparasitic before each swimming session","Wash and dry the skin properly after leaving the water","Apply DEET-based repellent to the legs before entering the water"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — treatment otherwise is symptomatic when the eruption occurs. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 91"},{"q":"A 39-year-old man in Arizona, where Lyme disease is not endemic, has fatigue and joint aches and asks to be tested. Which is the most appropriate response?","choices":["Testing is most helpful in exactly this situation — outside an endemic region with possibly consistent symptoms","Testing is not indicated at all, since Lyme disease occurs only in the northeastern states of the country","Testing should be deferred until an erythema migrans lesion has actually appeared","Testing should be replaced by an empirical course of doxycycline for two weeks"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay first, with Western blot more specific. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 77"}]};
